n outweigh Beth Macy's strong fundraising and Virginia's favorable early-voting trends for Democrats.", "watch": "Sustained Democratic early-voting margins through Election Day, or a late infusion of outside spending behind Macy, could tighten the race and put the Safe rating at risk.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "Incumbent Ben Cline (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Virginia's 6th District is structurally Republican, with a partisan lean of about twelve points toward the GOP, and Republican incumbent Ben Cline is seeking reelection. The only published poll, conducted by a Republican-affiliated firm in late September among likely voters, shows Cline ahead by roughly thirty points. Prediction markets price his chances at about ninety-four percent, and no expert ratings flag the district as competitive.\n\nDemocrat Beth Macy has run a serious campaign, ending September with more cash on hand than Cline, and October reporting highlights Democratic-leaning early voting across Virginia along with national attention to this race. Those signals argue against complete complacency, but the district's lean, the wide poll margin, and market pricing all point the same direction. A substantially larger Democratic wave than current indicators suggest would be required to put the outcome in doubt.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0375, "margin": -14.4}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0454, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0463, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0654, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0527, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0505, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0614, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0306, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0328, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0472, "margin": -13.5}, {"date": "20